WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:04.683
- Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.683 --> 00:00:08.060
Welcome to our daily homeroom,
for those of you all,

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:10.150
if this is the first
time you're joining us,

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.110
this is a livestream we're
doing on a daily basis.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.060
The catalyst was the school closures

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.920
that are now around the world,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.440
over 1.6 billion students
around the world,

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.770
who normally would be attending school,

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:24.230
are no longer doing that,
at least for the time being.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.410
And what we see our
responsibility as a not-for-profit

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:28.750
with a mission of providing a free

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:30.910
world-class education
for anyone, anywhere,

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.260
it's always been in service to teachers,

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.020
to parents, to students,
to make sure that folks

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:38.300
can have the best possible
learning experience.

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:39.890
And we realize that
especially in this time

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.360
of school closures, it's
our duty to step up,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.600
and figure out how we can support you,

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.480
the parents, the students,
the teachers out there,

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.985
how we can support school districts.

00:00:48.985 --> 00:00:49.910
I know we're gonna have a really

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.880
robust conversation about that today.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.390
And, I do want to remind everyone,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.320
we are a not-for-profit organization,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.040
and we are able to do what we do

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.420
because of philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.030
So if you're in the position to do so,

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.728
please think about
donating to Khan Academy.

00:01:05.728 --> 00:01:07.216
I want to give a special thanks

00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:10.250
to several corporate partners
who recently stepped up

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.010
to support these efforts
to keep Khan Academy going

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.759
so that we can keep everyone learning.

00:01:14.759 --> 00:01:19.130
Bank of America, AT&amp;T,
Google.org, and Novartis.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.000
If any of your corporations
are thinking about it,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.440
they should contact us as well.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.310
We were running at a deficit
even before this crisis hit

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.270
and now our costs have
gone up dramatically

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:32.045
to support our servers, which
are now 2-1/2, 3X normal load,

00:01:32.045 --> 00:01:34.480
and all the other programs that we would

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.858
like to support parents,
teachers, and students with.

00:01:36.858 --> 00:01:38.640
And so our deficits are even larger.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.810
So if you can, please support us.

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:44.810
So with that, I'd like
to introduce our guest,

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.150
Dr. Jesus Jara, who is the Superintendent

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.440
at Clark County School District.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.400
They are the fourth or fifth largest

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.890
school district in America.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:56.100
This is Greater Las
Vegas, for those of y'all

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.720
who might not be as
familiar with Clark County.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.630
And this is, I'm excited
about this conversation.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.560
We've partnered with Dr. Jara,
actually at Clark County,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.120
and in his previous world when
he was working in Orlando.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.690
But Dr. Jara, maybe a good place to start,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.180
how are you and your
family doing, first of all?

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.270
Before we get into the...

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.710
- Yeah, first before we get into the work,

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.390
well, first of all, Sal,
I just want to thank you

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.680
for giving me the opportunity
to share a little bit.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.920
We're all safe and sound at home.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.200
I've got two college kids
that have been working online

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.235
and doing some of the online
learning at both institutions.

00:02:30.235 --> 00:02:35.235
So we're just staying home,
staying home for Nevada

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.670
as our Governor has continued
to remind our folks.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.960
And learning a new
world of education here,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.780
and also administrating
the administration,

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.610
really, and helping our
hardworking teachers

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.880
and principals from our computers.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.870
So it's been a little bit of a challenge

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.750
in moving to this direction,
but we're doing well.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:56.583
Thank you.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.750
- And just going into that a little bit,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.610
I've been curious as, over the last,

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.640
everyone's been fighting their own crises

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.540
over the past four or five weeks,

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.690
but I'd love to hear it
from your perspective,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:11.660
as a superintendent of a
large complex school district,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.234
when did it get on your radar that

00:03:14.234 --> 00:03:16.050
something like this might happen?

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.065
And as best as you can,
kind of play it out?

00:03:18.065 --> 00:03:20.110
When did it become clear
it was going to happen,

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.210
how did you scenario plan,

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:24.270
what are you trying to optimize for now?

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.510
- Yeah, when it started,
we had early conversation,

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.143
I was having a discussion
with some of our partners

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:33.657
here on the strip and they said, hey,

00:03:33.657 --> 00:03:36.010
what are you guys doing
for the coronavirus?

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.990
And I immediately brought my team in,

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:41.950
and that was in late
February, to start putting in

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:45.930
and rolling out our emergency
management plan in place.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:49.230
As a school system we
always are dealing with

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:53.114
a lot of different issues,
and I recall the day I was,

00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:57.082
March 4th, I was up in
Carson City and Reno

00:03:57.082 --> 00:04:00.650
for a discussion, a
superintendent meeting,

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.780
and the County Commission Chair called

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:06.380
and immediately we had our first case.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.558
And I can tell you, playing
out from that first case

00:04:09.558 --> 00:04:14.300
from here in Las Vegas
to some of the issues

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:19.220
that just evolved rather
quickly in a matter of hours,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.410
to start just really
calming down the community

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.700
and learning and understanding
what that meant for children,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.540
'cause obviously the
first case was an adult,

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.740
but then it's the quarantine
and getting the guidance

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:35.173
from the CDC and then
really, from the first week,

00:04:35.173 --> 00:04:37.790
through the first week
and a half it's just,

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.430
from learning from the experts,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.260
learning from our medical
experts on processes,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:45.060
and putting and enacting our plan

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.130
that it seems like it's
been six, seven months,

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:51.349
where we're only a month,
a month and a week in,

00:04:51.349 --> 00:04:55.340
and really preparing for what
happens for school closure,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.970
and everything as a superintendent

00:04:57.970 --> 00:05:01.860
just evolved, and calling and working with

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.640
some of the long-standing superintendents

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.840
that have been in this
business to try to understand.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.249
But this is unlike any other,
unlike any other emergency.

00:05:12.249 --> 00:05:14.150
As you mentioned, I came from Florida,

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.060
and when we first started, and really

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:19.210
closing schools for hurricanes, right,

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:20.720
but there's always that endpoint,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:23.300
and how do you start
your continuity program?

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.700
This is different, this is so different

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:28.900
than preparing for a hurricane
or any other disaster.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.120
This has just been moving and learning

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.130
in unchartered waters.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:39.452
But then the impact that I
see here, that I see unlike

00:05:39.452 --> 00:05:43.540
any other place that I've
been at, it's just really

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.590
the impact, because our
kids and our families,

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:51.096
and then as we shut down, as the Governor,

00:05:51.096 --> 00:05:54.370
the leadership from the
Governor's office to shut down

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.830
the entire state in
education, all our facilities,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.240
then within two days, all the
businesses have shut down.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.940
So now, it's how do we start going through

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:08.056
all of that, providing the essentials?

00:06:08.056 --> 00:06:13.030
Our kids, 68% of our children
are on free and reduced lunch,

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.420
so it was preparing for how are we going

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.810
to feed our kids and our families?

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:22.810
So it just, just to
backpedal, moving to, again,

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.555
the essentials, the safety
first, getting them home,

00:06:27.555 --> 00:06:30.590
then feeding them, and then start moving

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:34.960
into our distance learning
education program.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.613
Like I said, it's been something that,

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.730
it's taken us a week or two to
start getting in the groove,

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.720
but then it always continues to evolve

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.810
and change and continue to learn

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.503
and make sure that our kids are engaged.

00:06:52.794 --> 00:06:54.120
- That's super useful.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.412
And for those of you all listening,

00:06:55.412 --> 00:06:57.170
if you're on Facebook or YouTube,

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:58.800
you can put questions
in the message boards.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.260
We have team members
who are looking at them,

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.080
and I'll try to surface those to Dr. Jara.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:03.532
This is a great opportunity
to ask questions

00:07:03.532 --> 00:07:06.100
of a superintendent who's running

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.910
a very large complex school district.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.830
If you're pretty much
in any other urban area,

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.160
the questions that you'd
ask your superintendent

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.280
are just as applicable as the
stuff that Dr. Jara is facing.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.570
But Dr. Jara, talk a little bit about,

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:18.011
more about that, because I think a lot of,

00:07:18.011 --> 00:07:20.200
for lack of a better word, civilians,

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.630
imagine, okay, the school
district, they teach some stuff,

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:25.390
maybe you have to go online a little bit,

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:27.560
but there's all these
other social services

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.665
that the school district
provides around lunches.

00:07:29.665 --> 00:07:32.222
There's also questions of
equity around internet access.

00:07:32.222 --> 00:07:34.910
How did that play out
with you and your team,

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.640
especially that first weekend, I remember,

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:37.890
when it started to become clear

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.700
that schools were going to have to close?

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:43.070
- Yeah, we started
preparing for that closure,

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.710
what is that closure gonna look like,

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.720
and then the team started putting together

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.670
learning opportunities, so
we can have paper, pencil,

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:51.266
when you look at the poverty level,

00:07:51.266 --> 00:07:55.180
we are the fifth largest
district, we have 320,000 kids.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:58.090
68% of them are free and reduced lunch.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:00.030
That means there's that level of poverty.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:03.120
And we did not have a
digital one-to-one device

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.547
as a school system and as a state,

00:08:04.547 --> 00:08:07.080
and so we had the
inequities there as well.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.140
So it was whatever learning opportunities

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:10.947
that we were gonna put out
there on the paper, pencil,

00:08:10.947 --> 00:08:13.960
we're also gonna give our kids access.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.720
So one of the things that
we originally started with,

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:21.470
it was really the main
essentials, 'cause as I mentioned,

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.300
our food insecurity is also very high,

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.990
so bringing in our food
department, our food service.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.858
We started with 15 sites
across our school system,

00:08:29.858 --> 00:08:32.860
to feed our community, to
feed our children, to come in,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:36.010
and at the same time as we're
doing that, then we also

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:39.870
put out our learning opportunities,
not only on the website,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.730
but then we started printing paper copies.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.290
So then you were getting a meal,

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.670
and it was just watching
and seeing our kids.

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:49.293
And I visited so many of them.

00:08:49.293 --> 00:08:53.260
Our parents were there, and
our families, and our kids,

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.450
and one of the things that was
very popular for the parents

00:08:56.450 --> 00:08:58.490
was the learning
opportunities, the homework,

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:00.657
the homework packets, not
so popular for our kids,

00:09:00.657 --> 00:09:04.273
but it was really a way
to keep them engaged,

00:09:04.273 --> 00:09:09.093
but their focus was again the
safety and the mental health.

00:09:10.067 --> 00:09:13.610
We moved into, from 15, very rapidly,

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:17.500
because we saw the gaps
across our 360 schools,

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.940
we moved, now we currently
have 38 different feeding sites

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:23.710
where we continue to
have the packets there.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.930
And then we started rolling
out our one-to-one devices,

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.630
our Chromebooks that we had in inventory,

00:09:30.630 --> 00:09:34.977
and then our first priority
was our high school seniors.

00:09:34.977 --> 00:09:37.563
We wanted to make sure that
our high school seniors,

00:09:37.563 --> 00:09:40.843
we were not going to have them suffer

00:09:40.843 --> 00:09:43.810
their graduation and their opportunities.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:46.470
So we started deploying Chromebooks,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.420
we're a Chromebook school system,

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:51.680
so we started deploying for
our high school seniors,

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:53.770
and then our credit-bearing
high school students,

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.090
for those that did not have devices.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:00.800
Also a big gap there,
we had about 64% of our,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:04.034
when we look at our
inventory, 64% of our students

00:10:04.034 --> 00:10:07.390
who had devices, the other ones did not.

00:10:07.390 --> 00:10:10.380
So immediately reallocated dollars

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.600
from the federal government
to purchase devices.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.810
So we purchased 46,000 devices that are

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:18.030
just coming in now, moving rather quickly.

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.270
Then we reached out to
our community partners,

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.379
Cox Communication to offer the Wi-Fi.

00:10:23.379 --> 00:10:28.179
Then we bought some, moving
rather swiftly, in purchasing

00:10:28.179 --> 00:10:31.730
the hot spots as well, so
we can distribute that.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:33.420
Again, that is all student-driven.

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.380
Our principals are doing a phenomenal job

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.360
of really working with our teachers.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.996
And then our teachers are now starting to,

00:10:38.996 --> 00:10:41.560
for those kids that have the opportunity

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.560
for the learning
opportunities, for the teachers

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.420
to start connecting
with our students online

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:52.420
through normal, their Google
Hangout and the Google Meetup

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.790
and some of those platforms
that we have available.

00:10:55.790 --> 00:10:58.196
And then in some cases, even phone calls,

00:10:58.196 --> 00:11:02.000
because again, the digital
divide for us was great.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.923
But I can tell you, this
community has stepped up

00:11:04.923 --> 00:11:09.000
significantly to provide
opportunities for our students.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.586
So those were some of the most immediate,

00:11:11.586 --> 00:11:15.901
and now we are monitoring,
as a matter of fact,

00:11:15.901 --> 00:11:19.063
as you mentioned, we're great partners.

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:22.690
Our assessment, now we're having

00:11:22.690 --> 00:11:26.390
deep conversations internally,
is what we do with,

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:29.086
now that we have everything,
I think, settled,

00:11:29.086 --> 00:11:32.330
when we look at our distribution
and our instruction,

00:11:32.330 --> 00:11:35.123
principals are now doing
virtual faculty meetings.

00:11:36.169 --> 00:11:40.100
So a lot of these things that
we're getting into a groove,

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.300
even, we're in Spring Break
this week, which is good,

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.490
it's letting us catch our
breath, to then kick-start.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.080
So now we're looking at, okay,

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:50.597
what are we gonna do now
for the next six weeks?

00:11:50.597 --> 00:11:53.480
We don't know whether we're
gonna come back to school.

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:56.380
It's now providing
professional development

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:58.170
for professional learning
for our teachers,

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.450
for our administrators, so
when our kids come back,

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:03.360
our children come back to
school, is how do we get

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.620
to close out the year, and
then really looking at the data

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:09.940
to start addressing some
of the gaps, and I know

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:14.210
that we're big partners
with you, with Khan Academy,

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:19.070
and MAP Accelerator, and
also our NWEA partner.

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.570
So we're looking at how we do that now

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:22.922
to get ready to start.

00:12:22.922 --> 00:12:26.420
You'll be proud, I'll tell
you, out of our numbers

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:30.030
in 3 through 8th, the number
I received this morning.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.465
out of 165,000 students
at that grade level,

00:12:33.465 --> 00:12:38.465
144,337 have been on MAP Accelerator

00:12:38.586 --> 00:12:43.586
at least doing some of
that individual support

00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:47.270
and really building up from what they had

00:12:48.932 --> 00:12:53.190
aligned to their RIT score on MAP Growth.

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.600
So we're really, at least, excited to see

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.850
that there's some engagement at home,

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.860
and I told the team, we need
to continue to push that out.

00:13:00.860 --> 00:13:02.840
It's not replacing the teacher,

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:05.870
but in these times, it's
giving us an opportunity

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.970
for some engagement in the classroom

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:11.143
and in the online classroom
at school and at home.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:13.640
- Wow, that's incredible
what y'all are doing.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:15.279
I don't think people fully appreciate

00:13:15.279 --> 00:13:18.100
some of what you just said,
I just want to underline it.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.120
You're thinking about feeding
hundreds of thousands of kids,

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.760
and then you deploying
internet access with devices

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:27.830
to sounds like tens of
thousands of households

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.820
in a matter of days, just
that is a major challenge.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:32.520
But from the way you're describing,

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.840
that's kind of the
baseline, and then from that

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:36.740
you can build on top of that virtually.

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:38.210
And for those of you who aren't familiar

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.560
with what Dr. Jara is referring
to, this MAP Accelerator,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.000
this is a partnership
we've done with the NWEA,

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:45.835
which is they provide the
MAP Growth Assessment,

00:13:45.835 --> 00:13:48.540
where that assessment
now acts as a diagnostic,

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.244
as a placement for work on Khan Academy.

00:13:50.244 --> 00:13:53.350
And Clark County is actually
one of the first districts

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:54.710
that we've partnered with around this.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.500
We did a pilot this past year,

00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:00.511
where Dr. Jara can see a
dashboard of what's going on,

00:14:00.511 --> 00:14:02.350
how the kids are using MAP Accelerator.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:03.860
I'm really heartened by those numbers.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:05.290
And this is an interesting idea

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:07.720
because this notion of online learning,

00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:09.741
we've obviously been talking
about the value of it

00:14:09.741 --> 00:14:12.290
to let students learn at their own pace,

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:14.550
remediate gaps, et cetera,
before this crisis,

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:16.010
but it is interesting how it's evolved,

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:18.283
that it's something you could
lean on maybe a little heavier

00:14:18.283 --> 00:14:21.644
when we're in a crisis
situation like this.

00:14:21.644 --> 00:14:24.340
I am getting a lot of questions here.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:29.340
Let's see, from YouTube, Craig Yen asks,

00:14:29.390 --> 00:14:32.633
how are you supporting your
teachers with distant learning?

00:14:33.891 --> 00:14:35.810
- Yeah, that's a great question,

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:37.800
and that's one of the things that we,

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:42.160
again, moving quickly,
into, in a week or so,

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.250
into a digital learning
platform for teachers,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.962
and that's one of the things that

00:14:45.962 --> 00:14:46.960
I just had a great
conversation this morning

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.210
with the team, is to now, continue

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:52.610
to support our teachers and
what does that look like?

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.380
So we're putting together a learning plan

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:59.137
for the next six weeks
and then for the summer.

00:14:59.137 --> 00:15:02.670
We haven't done that in the
past as an entire system,

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:05.441
So it's something that
now, in conversations with

00:15:05.441 --> 00:15:08.076
the Department of Education
here in the state of Nevada,

00:15:08.076 --> 00:15:11.140
is we're gonna put together
some of our experts

00:15:12.084 --> 00:15:14.070
across the state and start providing,

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.440
using another platform canvas,

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:19.150
to then provide some content for teachers.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.930
And then also, looking at
some of these model lessons

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.550
as well, one of the things that
we're gonna start requesting

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:29.654
from our teachers is to start
sharing some of their lessons

00:15:29.654 --> 00:15:33.030
so then we could then put
them on a platform for others.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:36.210
At the school level, our
principals have done a great job

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:40.925
in really doing some PLCs, it's
all online, doing some PLC--

00:15:40.925 --> 00:15:43.580
- [Sal] What does PLC stand for?

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.667
- Yep, professional learning communities

00:15:45.667 --> 00:15:48.970
within their grade level and specific

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:50.810
to the schools, led by the administrators

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:52.720
or their content experts at the school.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.030
So that's happening at
individualized school level.

00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:00.510
And then obviously, for providing
support for the teachers

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:04.060
as well during their
weekly faculty meeting.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:06.640
One of the other aspects that we're now,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.960
as we're looking at, is the
analyzing the use of data.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.497
So we're gonna start taking that time

00:16:11.497 --> 00:16:14.973
to do that now as we're
closing out the year.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.470
- And I have two related
questions, I'll combine them.

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:22.040
One from Facebook, Rebecca Nelson says,

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:24.350
do you foresee Clark County
embracing distance learning

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.410
for next school year, even if
the kids are able to return

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:28.430
to school, maybe modify
that as online learning

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.770
or some permutation, how
is the distance learning

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:32.220
changing the minds of educators?

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:35.390
And then related to that, Scott
Yang is asking on Facebook,

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.020
Dr. Jara, do you think
school can resume normally?

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:40.610
How do you think school will resume?

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.000
- Yeah, those are two great questions.

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:44.356
And that's what we've been,
we're challenging ourselves,

00:16:44.356 --> 00:16:47.070
'cause we're thinking our
kids are gonna come back,

00:16:47.070 --> 00:16:51.400
they're gonna enjoy this
new way of learning.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:53.980
So I've challenged the
team as we move forward

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:57.540
to look at how are we going
to capitalize on where we are,

00:16:57.540 --> 00:16:59.680
to provide opportunities
for distance learning?

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:01.315
So it's a conversation we're having.

00:17:01.315 --> 00:17:04.808
I think as a district, it's stretched us,

00:17:04.808 --> 00:17:08.120
our mindset, to then start moving forward

00:17:08.120 --> 00:17:10.430
to provide different opportunities.

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:13.460
So do I predict it's going
to be a lot different?

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:17.735
I believe it is, and we're
going to have to evolve

00:17:17.735 --> 00:17:20.893
and move quickly to be
able to get to that.

00:17:21.871 --> 00:17:26.100
So it's gonna be different
and maybe more project-based.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.300
And these are the conversations

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:30.163
that we're having now as
a team for the next year.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:32.910
- And are there conversations happening,

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.490
or is it just too far ahead,
are people talking about

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.553
what if the virus picks
back up in the Fall,

00:17:39.807 --> 00:17:42.690
or we loosen social distancing
and it picks back up?

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:43.840
How are y'all thinking about that?

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:45.760
Or is that just too far out right now?

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.507
- Well, Sal, this is why I love you.

00:17:48.507 --> 00:17:50.130
You push me to think all the time.

00:17:50.130 --> 00:17:52.710
We were thinking that we
may come back to school,

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:54.260
when we come back to school in August,

00:17:54.260 --> 00:17:56.100
when we do that, we weren't

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.573
thinking about having to come back.

00:18:01.511 --> 00:18:02.940
It's a good question to ask.

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:05.350
We were thinking that once
we come back when it's safe,

00:18:05.350 --> 00:18:08.560
we'll just start over, it's
just gonna be different

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:11.200
and innovative, and I
think that's where we are.

00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:14.480
But we'll just, at least
now, as I say to the team,

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:17.248
we know how to shut down a school district

00:18:17.248 --> 00:18:19.727
rather quickly, we can start it back up.

00:18:19.727 --> 00:18:23.230
But not yet, not yet as to coming back

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:27.570
and having to close again
because of the virus.

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.350
- Yeah, and I can't imagine the complexity

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:31.850
you have to deal with,
on a day-to-day basis,

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:34.580
much less through a process like this.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:36.840
So you touched on it a little bit,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:38.330
but there's two questions,
and this is a question

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:39.530
I've been getting a lot too,

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:41.230
and I am curious to get in more depth.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:43.710
From YouTube user name July is asking,

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:45.460
how can you make sure that all kids

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:47.450
in your district have a
chance to learn online?

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:48.430
Do they have necessary equipment

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:50.220
like laptops, tablets, or internet?

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:53.793
And then from YouTube
Sixtus106 asks, Dr. Jara,

00:18:53.793 --> 00:18:56.390
what are you doing to support
kids who don't have internet?

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:59.630
You mentioned a little bit
this roll out of Chromebooks,

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:01.805
you got more Chromebooks or
devices, you're trying to,

00:19:01.805 --> 00:19:04.740
where are we, do all of
your kids now have access,

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:05.910
or are there still a subset that don't,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:07.480
and how are you handling that?

00:19:07.480 --> 00:19:10.420
- So our first week, when we
took attendance our first week,

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:14.260
which was three weeks ago,
we have 21, we were still

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:19.260
looking for, back then,
March 23rd through the 27th,

00:19:19.275 --> 00:19:22.190
we were missing 21% of our students

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:23.730
that we had not been able to contact.

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.440
So now we're putting our social services,

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:27.920
we're putting our social workers,

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:30.040
we're putting call-outs
into the community.

00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:32.400
If you have not heard from
us, at least back then,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.610
is that you need to contact
the school and then continue

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:37.061
that conversation as we
continue to take attendance.

00:19:37.061 --> 00:19:41.363
There is still the huge
digital divide that we have,

00:19:41.363 --> 00:19:44.460
and we started, we have
our Public Ed Foundation

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:47.260
as well that has in our
community, very giving community,

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:52.110
that has started a campaign
to raise money for us,

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:54.745
not only for the device,
but for the hot spots,

00:19:54.745 --> 00:19:58.150
but also for the professional
learning for the students,

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:00.300
for the parents, and for our teachers.

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:03.493
So it's a work in progress
to be quite candid with you.

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:07.163
- And related to that, we
have a bunch of questions

00:20:07.163 --> 00:20:10.830
about the remainder of the
year, Facebook, Yolanda Brinez,

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:15.362
Facebook, Denise Puckett, on
YouTube, Liana Garcia Crespo,

00:20:15.362 --> 00:20:17.568
they're all asking some version of,

00:20:17.568 --> 00:20:19.917
is it for sure that schools are gonna

00:20:19.917 --> 00:20:22.310
be remained closed through
the end of the year?

00:20:22.310 --> 00:20:25.680
And then a related question
from Facebook, Tammy Harveston

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:28.523
is asking, will graduations
be canceled or postponed?

00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:31.850
- So we're waiting.

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:33.107
When the Governor closed the schools,

00:20:33.107 --> 00:20:36.506
it was it'll be depending, if
we open, it'll be depending on

00:20:36.506 --> 00:20:39.670
the State chief health officer
to make that determination.

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:41.720
We've seen others across the country

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:44.428
that have made the
decisions not to reopen.

00:20:44.428 --> 00:20:48.120
So it's a wait-and-see, so we
don't have that information.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:50.960
I've asked for at least
a week lead time for us

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:54.460
to be able to reopen, it
just depends on where we are.

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:56.530
As a matter of fact, having conversations

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:58.778
this morning with the
team and I'm calling in,

00:20:58.778 --> 00:21:02.680
next Wednesday, I will
be meeting, virtually,

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.940
with all our senior class
presidents from our schools,

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:10.110
we've got 49 high schools, to get ideas,

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:12.906
'cause we have some ideas
about how we do graduation.

00:21:12.906 --> 00:21:15.780
Graduation is in May,
I don't foresee that,

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:20.780
because our partner at University
of Nevada Las Vegas here,

00:21:21.366 --> 00:21:24.630
UNLV, is where we host,
and they're closed,

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:28.153
and they've canceled their
graduations or postponed them.

00:21:28.153 --> 00:21:30.260
So we don't have the facility.

00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:33.700
So we're gonna call out our seniors, I am,

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.860
to talk to them and get
some ideas from them,

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:39.340
'cause my greatest idea is not,

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:41.634
I'm not at their appropriate age group,

00:21:41.634 --> 00:21:45.430
so I'm gonna get some of their
ideas to see how we do this.

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:47.170
- Yeah, and what about credit generally?

00:21:47.170 --> 00:21:48.890
Is it still kind of a work-in-progress,

00:21:48.890 --> 00:21:51.160
things like grades, is there a way that,

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:53.550
you don't have a point
of view on that yet?

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.020
- We do, the State just
released some guidance,

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:58.410
and working with all superintendents,

00:21:58.410 --> 00:21:59.887
so we're working together.

00:21:59.887 --> 00:22:02.700
We are going to our third marking period.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:04.840
We opened up, which was right at close,

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:08.003
as soon as we were finishing
and we ended up closing school.

00:22:08.003 --> 00:22:10.610
So we've opened up the
third marking period

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:13.700
for all our high schools and
our schools for credit-bearing

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:15.902
and across-the-board
secondary schools as well,

00:22:15.902 --> 00:22:20.040
for students to make up any assignments

00:22:20.040 --> 00:22:25.040
that they missed or wanted
to redo, so that's available.

00:22:26.170 --> 00:22:28.850
And now we're having the conversations

00:22:28.850 --> 00:22:32.400
about what we do for the
fourth marking period

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:35.050
and graduation requirements,
the State has left it up to us.

00:22:35.050 --> 00:22:37.470
So next week we'll be meeting with

00:22:37.470 --> 00:22:40.470
our senior high school
principals, and put some of those

00:22:40.470 --> 00:22:44.805
requirements out for
our high school seniors.

00:22:44.805 --> 00:22:47.510
And then also having conversations

00:22:47.510 --> 00:22:49.980
with our higher Ed partners,
because I didn't want

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:52.640
our students because of this,
because of this pandemic,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:57.457
to be penalized for GPA and
scholarship opportunities.

00:22:57.457 --> 00:22:59.820
So we're looking and
we're always gonna side

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:03.600
on the side of the students,
and it's not their fault,

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:07.170
and it's something that we need

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:09.070
to find ways to address and help them.

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:12.850
- Yeah, and related to
everything we're talking about,

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:15.440
really, from YouTube,
Oshitosh Tiwari is asking,

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:18.590
how do you think the education
curriculum and syllabus will,

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:21.050
I think he's saying,
catch up, after COVID-19?

00:23:21.050 --> 00:23:22.650
He's referring to this issue of,

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:24.810
normally you have a summer
slide, three months,

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:26.900
of not only going to
school, but potentially

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:29.260
forgetting or lost, now
you have five months.

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:31.130
We've seen some data that that might lead,

00:23:31.130 --> 00:23:32.940
if kids aren't engaged
over that five months,

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.636
to as much as a year of lost learning.

00:23:35.636 --> 00:23:37.543
So kids are gonna come at
the beginning of the year

00:23:37.543 --> 00:23:39.920
with even bigger gaps, bigger variation,

00:23:39.920 --> 00:23:42.259
are y'all, what's your thinking there?

00:23:42.259 --> 00:23:45.801
- Yeah, that's a great
question and great comment.

00:23:45.801 --> 00:23:47.880
I know that data was released,

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.116
NWEA released their data this morning

00:23:50.116 --> 00:23:53.460
on their COVID-19 slide,
which is startling.

00:23:53.460 --> 00:23:56.450
I've been on the phone, I
was with them on the phone

00:23:56.450 --> 00:24:01.450
yesterday and last week with
Chris Minnich and his team.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:03.130
One of the things that we're looking at

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:06.260
is to prepare for the restart and using

00:24:06.260 --> 00:24:09.110
some of the federal stimulus
dollars that are coming in,

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:11.320
which is going to be very helpful.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.150
Is it gonna be, so the team is looking at

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:17.710
the standards that we've
missed in this marking period,

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:21.482
so then how do we embed
them in the first semester?

00:24:21.482 --> 00:24:26.482
Is it extending the day, it's
opportunities in the summer?

00:24:29.300 --> 00:24:33.440
So we'll have a plan in
place in the coming week

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.290
on what we're going to be
able to do for our students

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:39.433
to catch them up because
it really is startling.

00:24:42.385 --> 00:24:44.500
And when the data, when I looked at it,

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:48.360
and the pushback, not a
pushback, I think it was really,

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:52.480
when I looked at the data on
the average student from NWEA,

00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:55.472
and I think the equity
gaps, and that's what

00:24:55.472 --> 00:24:58.220
keeps me up at night,
because I think when you look

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:02.390
at the equity caps for our
children living in poverty,

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:03.710
our English language learners,

00:25:03.710 --> 00:25:06.840
I think it's gonna be a
greater slide for them.

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:08.590
- Yeah. I just want you
to know, you're getting

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:10.320
a lot of very positive feedback

00:25:10.320 --> 00:25:12.480
from several folks on
YouTube, on Facebook.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:14.830
On Facebook, Shelly Fox
said, I love that you are

00:25:14.830 --> 00:25:16.880
reaching out to seniors
for input and ideas.

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.440
Thank you for your leadership.

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:21.930
And then from YouTube,
BeautifulBecks wrote,

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:23.240
I think it's amazing how he's getting

00:25:23.240 --> 00:25:26.020
student input for the seniors,
I hope my school does that.

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:28.600
So a lot of kudos there, Dr. Jara.

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:29.593
- Well thank you.

00:25:31.320 --> 00:25:33.390
- And so there's another question here,

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:34.720
let me see if I can find it again,

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:36.770
it was about, it sounds like a parent,

00:25:36.770 --> 00:25:39.330
there you go, from YouTube, Jen Mills,

00:25:39.330 --> 00:25:40.640
and this is a question
I've been getting a lot,

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.750
although I have very
little expertise here,

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:44.550
how are you handling special education?

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:47.360
My son is autistic and our
district is not organized.

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:49.800
How are y'all thinking about that?

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:54.580
- Well, just to defend of the
district, I think there was

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:57.855
a lot of different
information coming to us early

00:25:57.855 --> 00:26:01.300
from the U.S. Department of
Education, and it was just

00:26:01.300 --> 00:26:05.810
really moving very, very
swiftly and changing.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:08.280
So my team did a great job in working with

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:10.210
the Council of Great City Schools.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:12.963
We put a document
together and it's really,

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:17.610
we're using the virtual world, obviously,

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:21.650
'cause, as we said, we
have a stay at home order,

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:25.191
so our special education educators

00:26:25.191 --> 00:26:28.810
are reaching out through,
whether they have

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.820
Google Meets or whether
it's video conferencing,

00:26:33.820 --> 00:26:36.210
of if not, there's no
technology, is a phone call,

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:39.130
video conferencing, to
provide the services.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:42.020
I'll be honest, some of
the bigger challenges

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:43.752
that we're seeing is when we have,

00:26:43.752 --> 00:26:47.440
we can do the support
there from our teachers

00:26:48.522 --> 00:26:51.850
and our support staff that helps our kids,

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:53.150
one of the biggest challenges

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.193
that we're seeing is when we have

00:26:55.193 --> 00:26:58.200
the occupational therapy
and the physical therapy

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:00.260
and we have to put our
hands on our students,

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:02.915
so that's one of the things
that we're being challenged.

00:27:02.915 --> 00:27:07.200
So I want to, really,
for the school system,

00:27:07.200 --> 00:27:10.330
wherever you are, it's
just been a moving target

00:27:10.330 --> 00:27:12.363
and a challenge for us as well.

00:27:13.290 --> 00:27:15.158
- Yeah, I can imagine.

00:27:15.158 --> 00:27:16.930
So there's so many questions.

00:27:16.930 --> 00:27:20.098
We're gonna have to
have you on again soon.

00:27:20.098 --> 00:27:23.810
But maybe to close out,
there's a lot of doom and gloom

00:27:23.810 --> 00:27:25.995
out in the world today, and a
lot of unanswered questions.

00:27:25.995 --> 00:27:29.780
But you kind of hinted at
maybe there's silver linings,

00:27:29.780 --> 00:27:31.940
that as we go through this
very suboptimal situation,

00:27:31.940 --> 00:27:33.130
there might be things that we come out

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.970
maybe better for, or different at least.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:39.082
Curious to get your
thoughts, what's your message

00:27:39.082 --> 00:27:42.810
for any parents and teachers
and students listening

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:45.280
from Clark County, or as a proxy

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:46.840
for other peoples' superintendents,

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:48.640
what would you like to tell them?

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:51.810
- Well, just be patient,
which is a message

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:55.030
I've shared with our
communities, Sal and everyone,

00:27:55.030 --> 00:27:57.850
is just be patient, this
is unprecedented times.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:28:00.070
We don't know, and we're
gonna make mistakes.

00:28:00.070 --> 00:28:05.070
It's hard, as I'll tell you,
as I do and hope and I know,

00:28:06.308 --> 00:28:08.490
my colleague superintendents,
they're gonna make

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:12.030
the best decisions that they
think is the right decision

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.157
on behalf of their families and students.

00:28:14.157 --> 00:28:16.803
So just be a little
patient, it's hard work.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:22.720
As I see the future, I think
this is gonna force us to say,

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.430
you know, education is
gonna look different.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:28.409
What different, what that
looks like, I don't know.

00:28:28.409 --> 00:28:32.620
Our teachers, I'll tell you,
are hardworking teachers,

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:34.782
and I think our teachers, our educators,

00:28:34.782 --> 00:28:39.250
have jumped right in, and
on behalf of our kids,

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:40.620
trying to do the best they can.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:43.950
So looking forward to the
future and what that looks like,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:47.080
we don't know what that
is, but it'll be exciting

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:49.663
for our kids, that's the
silver lining as I see it.

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:52.606
- Yeah, well, Dr. Jara, as always,

00:28:52.606 --> 00:28:55.610
it's great to talk to you,
it's an honor to have y'all

00:28:55.610 --> 00:28:57.570
as one of our close
partners at Khan Academy.

00:28:57.570 --> 00:29:00.890
And stay safe and healthy, and
keep doing your great work.

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:02.320
- I will, thank you, Sal, I appreciate it.

00:29:02.320 --> 00:29:03.370
Thanks for having me.

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:05.674
- Thank you.

00:29:05.674 --> 00:29:07.699
Well, everyone, I thought that was

00:29:07.699 --> 00:29:11.583
a super valuable update from Dr. Jara.

00:29:11.583 --> 00:29:13.374
I just want to thank all
of y'all for joining.

00:29:13.374 --> 00:29:15.110
We hope to continue to do this

00:29:15.110 --> 00:29:17.740
throughout the closures
and maybe beyond, everyday.

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:19.021
Keep your questions coming.

00:29:19.021 --> 00:29:22.410
And I'll just give another
reminder, we are not-for-profit.

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.670
If you're in a position to
do so, please think about

00:29:24.670 --> 00:29:28.110
donating to Khan Academy, there is a URL

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:31.170
someplace on this screen,
khanacademy.org/donate.

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:32.740
And I'll also say, just speaking

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:34.590
as one human being to another,

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:37.430
whenever I meet folks like Dr. Jara.

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:39.510
and we had David Coleman
on a couple of days ago,

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:41.410
and Bill Gates a couple
of days before that,

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:43.550
you just see how a lot of folks are

00:29:43.550 --> 00:29:45.249
stepping up and doing what they can.

00:29:45.249 --> 00:29:48.300
As hard as this situation
is, I've been seeing

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:50.640
a lot of kind of the
best of humanity step up,

00:29:50.640 --> 00:29:52.740
and we're all going through this together.

00:29:53.811 --> 00:29:56.660
And it's great to be able to
connect with y'all in this way,

00:29:56.660 --> 00:29:57.880
and hopefully it's making you feel

00:29:57.880 --> 00:29:58.890
a little less socially distanced.

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:01.170
It's definitely helping me with that.

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:04.003
And so with that, I will
see y'all all tomorrow.

